## Why This Question Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design real-time, multilingual NLP systems that balance privacy, governance, latency constraints, and safe escalation.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming NLP with strict latency budgets
- Privacy-by-design with on-device preprocessing
- Multilingual toxic text detection and debiasing
- Drift detection and governance hooks for human escalation
- Operational readiness: rollout, monitoring, and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: redaction + toxicity routing
function processMessage(msg) {
  // Stage 1: PII redaction
  const redacted = redactPII(msg.text);

  // Stage 2: Toxicity classification
  const prediction = await classifier.predict(redacted);

  // Stage 3: Policy gate routing
  if (prediction.score > HIGH_RISK_THRESHOLD) {
    queueForHumanReview({ ...msg, redactedText: redacted, prediction });
  } else {
    publishMessage({ ...msg, redactedText: redacted, prediction });
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design latency-aware, privacy-preserving, multilingual systems that justify decisions rather than just detect. Emphasizes RAG, policy integration, and drift handling across many languages.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) vs pure classifiers
- On-device first-pass with quantized transformers
- Privacy: differential privacy, encrypted policy KB, secure inference
- Multilingual handling and code-switching
- Drift detection, rollout strategies, rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo architecture sketch
class Detector {
  constructor(){ /* load on-device model */ }
  async predict(msg){
    const lang = identifyLanguage(msg);
    const local = await runOnDevice(msg, lang);
    const refs = await fetchPolicyRefs(msg, lang); // retrieval
    const rationale = buildRAG(refs, local);
    const cloudOut = await cloudInfer(msg, rationale);
    return { label: cloudOut.label, rationale };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate policy-ref quality and latency separately?
- How would you handle a new language with minimal data and ensure privacy material?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end deployment of multilingual fact-checking with RAG, a practical need for large product support teams. It blends privacy, latency, and explainability in production.

## Key Concepts
- Knowledge graphs and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
- Multilingual encoders and cross-lingual alignment
- Citations, confidence scoring, audit logging
- KB versioning, canaries, rollback, drift monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code illustrating the RAG flow
async function veracityPipeline(query, lang) {
  const claim = extractClaim(query);
  const facts = await retrieveFacts(claim, lang); // KG + external sources
  const resp = composeAnswer(claim, facts, lang);
  return { resp, citations: facts.map(f => f.source), confidence: aggregateConfidence(facts) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle conflicting sources or low-confidence claims?
- How would you evaluate latency vs. accuracy during rollout?
